AB  - The integration of GenAI into higher education risks promoting passive information acceptance over critical thinking (CT), a challenge that is acutely felt in ill-structured problem-solving contexts such as engineering design. While cognitive scaffolds are recognized as a solution, limited research provides process-oriented evidence on how they mediate student engagement with GenAI. Currently, typical GenAI integration often leads to cognitive offloading where students bypass critical evaluation. To counter this automation bias, this study introduced a novel instructional framework that integrated the Preparation for Future Collaboration (PFC) paradigm with the Target-Criteria-Source-Analogy (TCSA) cognitive strategy to structure GenAI-supported learning. By analyzing the critical thinking patterns of 77 engineering undergraduates across high-, medium-, and low-performing groups, Epistemic Network Analysis (ENA) identified distinct cognitive co-occurrence patterns across GenAI-supported preparation and subsequent AI-free collaboration. High-performing students showed stronger connections among Analysis, Evaluation, and Self-regulation, a pattern we interpretively describe as a “Triangulation Loop,” whereas low-performing students showed stronger connections between Interpretation and Analysis, described as a “Descriptive Loop.” The latter pattern was consistent with greater reliance on AI-generated information. Within the integrated PFC-TCSA instructional design, these observed patterns were characterized by stronger evaluative and self-regulatory co-occurrences, providing practical implications for designing human–AI collaboration.
