AB  - The integration of Generative AI (GenAI) in higher education represents a paradigmatic shift from automation to augmentation. It fundamentally transforms how students engage with technology. But how do students' interactions with GenAItools shape their learning, critical thinking, and ethical engagement? Our qualitative study examines how 101 postgraduate students at an Indian university interact with GenAI tools (ChatGPT, Bard, Bing Chat, and Perplexity.ai) through contextualized learning experiences in supply chain analytics and manufacturing logistics. Using grounded theory analysis of open-ended interviews, we identified six interconnected themes spanning technological, cognitive and ethical dimensions as a result of students navigating AI-mediated learning environments. Our findings showed that students were able to develop sophisticated “distributed creativity” and "augmented metacognition," enabling them to negotiate complex relationships between AI assistance and intellectual ownership. Rather than passive consumption, students demonstrated emerging AI literacy, whereby they were able to critically evaluate AI output, recognize its limitations while also maintain authorial control. These insights illuminate evidence-based strategies for fostering AI literacy while preserving academic integrity, enabling educators to responsibly harness GenAI's transformative potential.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - How high-school pressure and autonomy support are linked to dual AI learning pathways: A cross-contextual SEM analysis
AU  - Qinjie, Shen
AU  - Arunrugstichai, Wynn
JO  - Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence
VL  - 10
SP  - 100621
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/06/01/
SN  - 2666-920X
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.caeai.2026.100621
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666920X26000834
KW  - Generative AI learning
KW  - Dual-pathway model
KW  - Self-determination theory
AB  - Generative AI tools are rapidly becoming embedded in students' everyday learning, yet students' AI use varies from constructive support to compulsive dependence. Drawing on Self-Determination Theory and the Hook model, this study proposes a dual-pathway framework examining how retrospective reports of high-school learning climate relate to university students’ GenAI-use patterns and perceived AI-supported learning quality through motivational and behavioral relations. Using cross-sectional survey data from university students with different high-school backgrounds and current university contexts (overall N = 508; China-HS group n = 305 and non-China-HS group n = 203; China-Uni n = 193 and Thailand-Uni n = 315), we tested the model using structural equation modeling and multi-group analyses. Results were consistent with a pressure-compulsive pathway: high-school pressure was associated with higher controlled motivation, which was in turn associated with higher compulsive AI use, lower well-being, and lower perceived AI-supported learning quality. In parallel, results supported an autonomy-adaptive pathway: high-school autonomy support and current university autonomy support were associated with higher autonomous motivation and planned trigger readiness, which were associated with more adaptive AI use, higher perceived academic performance and well-being, and higher perceived AI-supported learning quality. The model explained substantial variance in key endogenous outcomes. Scalar-level measurement invariance supported meaningful cross-group comparisons, and multi-group analyses suggested that the overall pattern of associations was broadly similar across the sampled high-school and university groupings, with differences concentrated in specific links. Overall, the findings suggest that motivational climate may be an important correlate of whether GenAI is incorporated into learning in more adaptive versus more reliance-oriented ways within the China, Thailand-linked contexts examined in this study.
ER  - 

